WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.102 --> 00:00:02.310
JENNY BROWN: Back to basics
for "Cloud Storage Bytes"

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:04.050
means back to buckets.

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:05.550
In this episode,
we'll take a look

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:06.990
at how different
types of buckets

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:08.620
impact your performance.

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:11.340
We'll bust some myths and
optimize our storage, just

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:12.045
like old times.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.010
Something we hear a
lot on "Storage Bytes"

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:20.840
is, what type of
bucket should I be

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:23.210
using for the best performance?

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:25.130
And as we've
discussed previously,

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:27.510
Cloud Storage has a few
different bucket options.

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:29.420
But when talking
quick retrieval,

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:31.970
we're going to focus on
multi-regional and regional

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:32.810
buckets.

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:34.970
For a quick refresh
of what that means,

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:37.280
a regional cloud storage
bucket guarantees

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:40.460
that all data in that
bucket lies in a specified

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:43.820
region, which is really
important in situations

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:47.660
where you know where your client
or on-prem systems are located

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:49.550
and you want to get
your GCS buckets

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:53.510
as close to those client
systems as possible.

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:55.670
Multi-regional storage,
on the other hand,

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:59.270
guarantees two replicas of
your data which are geodiverse.

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:01.940
That means they're a
minimum of 100 miles apart.

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:03.800
This may result in
data being moved

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:07.310
between regions for better
latency and availability.

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:09.530
More importantly,
multi-regional buckets

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:13.310
heavily leverage edge caching
and CDNs to provide the content

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:14.960
to end users.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:17.030
To illustrate some
real life scenarios

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:20.010
and better understand how these
systems respond under stress,

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:21.630
we'll set up a test.

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:24.230
Upload a 2 megabyte file
to a bunch of regional

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:27.020
and multi-regional buckets,
and then fetch that asset--

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:31.250
with caching disabled--
from a VM in us-west1.

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:33.540
We've got a few
different takeaways here.

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:35.690
Firstly, fetches
in the same region,

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:38.100
or in a sub region
of the multi-region,

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:41.700
perform pretty solidly,
just as we would expect.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.340
But when you're
fetching from a region

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:45.140
noticeably far
from where you are,

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:48.120
you really do notice the
drop in response time.

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:50.930
And this is where
multi-regional buckets shine.

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:53.810
By duplicating that data
to a geodiverse region,

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:55.490
there is now a second
instance that's

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:58.550
likely to be much closer
to the final destination.

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:01.160
You see that the latency
drops, and often it's

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:02.760
pretty significant.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:04.700
The biggest takeaway
here is that there's

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:06.500
no specific
performance difference

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:09.710
for the classification of
the buckets themselves.

00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:12.980
The performance is dominated
by the latency and physical

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:17.090
distance between the client
and the cloud storage bucket.

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:20.570
Practically applied, we can
conclude that if caching is on

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:23.795
and your access volume is high
enough to take advantage of it,

00:02:23.795 --> 00:02:25.670
there's not a huge
difference between the two

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:29.240
offerings due to Google's
powerful CDN environment.

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:32.300
If caching is off or if the
access volume is low enough

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.100
that you can't take
advantage of it,

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:37.970
then performance overhead is
dominated primarily by physics.

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:41.450
You should really aim to get the
assets as close to the clients

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:44.990
as possible, trading off for
your data's needs around cost,

00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:47.430
redundancy, and consistency.

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:49.820
There's plenty more to come
about optimizing performance,

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:51.195
but we're going
to do you a favor

00:02:51.195 --> 00:02:53.197
and save that for
another episode.

00:02:53.197 --> 00:02:55.280
If you'd like to know more,
go ahead and check out

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:56.810
the links in the description.

00:02:56.810 --> 00:03:00.170
And don't forget to subscribe,
give us a like, and as always,

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:03.470
tell us what you want to learn
about Google Cloud Storage.

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:05.870
Thank you for joining us
for this quick byte of Cloud

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:06.440
Storage.

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:08.590
See you next time.

